---
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import EngineAnalyzer from '../../../components/engine-analyzer/EngineAnalyzer.astro';
import '../../../styles/sound-analyzer.css';
const title = 'Engine Sound Analyzer — Live Spectrum, Harmonics & RPM References | WorkshopGirl';
const description = 'Inspect engine sound live in your browser. View frequencies, harmonics, optional RPM and firing references, and before/after snapshots without recording.';
const canonical = 'https://workshopgirl.com/tools/engine-sound-analyzer/';
---
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" /><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <title>{title}</title><meta name="description" content={description} /><link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
  <meta property="og:title" content={title} /><meta property="og:description" content={description} /><meta property="og:type" content="website" /><meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
  <meta property="og:image" content="https://workshopgirl.com/images/workshop-girl/workshop-girl-hero.png" /><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" /><link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
</head><body class="engine-page">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to engine sound analyzer</a><SiteHeader current="tools" />
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1"><div class="hero"><a class="eyebrow" href="/tools/">WorkshopGirl / Digital workbench</a><h1>Engine Sound <span>Analyzer.</span></h1><p>Listen live. Inspect the frequencies. See what changed.</p><span class="privacy-note">No recording required · local audio processing · observations, not fault diagnosis</span></div>
    <EngineAnalyzer />
    <section class="guide" aria-labelledby="engine-guide"><span class="eyebrow">Measure with context</span><h2 id="engine-guide">A reference is a starting point.</h2>
      <h3>Start with live sound</h3><p>Start the microphone and inspect the waveform, spectrum and spectrogram immediately. Capture A and B without recording or stopping. Optional recording and audio files use the same DSP core as the <a href="/tools/sound-analyzer/">General Sound Analyzer</a>.</p>
      <h3>RPM references are calculated, not measured</h3><p>Shaft frequency is RPM ÷ 60: 1800 RPM gives 30 Hz. Dashed markers show 0.5×, 1×, 2×, 3× and 4× that reference. Manual RPM is not synchronized order tracking, and matching a marker does not establish which part made a sound.</p>
      <h3>Firing frequency depends on the engine</h3><p>For a conventional evenly firing four-stroke engine, the reference is RPM ÷ 60 × cylinders ÷ 2. For a two-stroke, it is RPM ÷ 60 × cylinders. Uneven firing, cylinder deactivation and unusual layouts or operating modes can differ.</p>
      <h3>Harmonics show structure</h3><p>A supplied reference defines 1×–5× harmonic targets. Nearby spectral peaks are matched within one FFT bin. Very low references may be unresolved by the current window. A strong higher harmonic or missing fundamental does not automatically identify a fault. Relative levels are referenced to the strongest matched harmonic; matched-band power is not a distortion or health score.</p>
      <h3>Make comparisons repeatable</h3><p>Keep the same phone position and distance, similar RPM and load, and the same analysis settings. Avoid wind and avoid touching or blocking the microphone. Phone automatic gain and noise processing can change levels. A/B warns when recorded conditions differ; unrecorded conditions still need your attention.</p>
      <h3>Digital levels, honest limits</h3><p>Levels are digital dBFS, not calibrated acoustic dB SPL. Phone microphones are not laboratory microphones. This tool cannot guarantee fault detection, establish engine health, or replace professional vibration analysis. Brief impacts or rapid changes may require more specialized acquisition and analysis.</p>
      <h3>Keep clear of the machinery</h3><p>Keep phones, hands, cables, clothing and other objects clear of belts, fans, pulleys, moving components and hot exhaust parts. Measure from a safe, stationary position. Do not reach into machinery to improve a reading.</p>
      <h3>Your audio stays in this browser</h3><p>No microphone or audio data is uploaded. This page has no analytics or cloud analysis. Snapshots remain in memory only. Stop, hide the tab or leave the page to release the microphone. Save an optional recording with Download clip if you want to keep it.</p>
      <p><a href="/tools/sound-analyzer/">Open General Sound Analyzer</a> · <a href="/tools/">All WorkshopGirl tools</a></p>
    </section>
  </main><SiteFooter />
</body></html>
